"author":[{"fullName":"Sahu PK","lastName":"Sahu","firstName":"Pushpendra Kumar","initials":"PK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"From the Laboratory of Chromatin Biology, Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Science Education and Research Bhopal, 462066 Madhya Pradesh, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Tomar RS","lastName":"Tomar","firstName":"Raghuvir Singh","initials":"RS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3574-7839"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"From the Laboratory of Chromatin Biology, Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Science Education and Research Bhopal, 462066 Madhya Pradesh, India rst@iiserb.ac.in."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3574-7839"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","related_data","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"294","journalIssueId":2794463,"dateOfPublication":"2019 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2019,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Biol Chem","title":"The Journal of biological chemistry","essn":"1083-351X","issn":"0021-9258","isoabbreviation":"J Biol Chem","nlmid":"2985121R"},"printPublicationDate":"2019-03-01"},"pubYear":"2019","pageInfo":"3837-3852","abstractText":"Cantharidin (CTD) is a potent anticancer small molecule produced by several species of blister beetle. It has been a traditional medicine for the management of warts and tumors for many decades. CTD suppresses tumor growth by inducing apoptosis, cell cycle arrest, and DNA damage and inhibits protein phosphatase 2 phosphatase activator (PP2A) and protein phosphatase 1 (PP1). CTD also alters lipid homeostasis, cell wall integrity, endocytosis, adhesion, and invasion in yeast cells. In this study, we identified additional molecular targets of CTD using a <i>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</i> strain that expresses a cantharidin resistance gene (<i>CRG1</i>), encoding a SAM-dependent methyltransferase that methylates and inactivates CTD. We found that CTD specifically affects phosphatidylethanolamine (PE)-associated functions that can be rescued by supplementing the growth media with ethanolamine (ETA). CTD also perturbed endoplasmic reticulum (ER) homeostasis and cell wall integrity by altering the sorting of glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI)-anchored proteins. A CTD-dependent genetic interaction profile of <i>CRG1</i> revealed that the activity of the lipid phosphatase cell division control protein 1 (Cdc1) in GPI-anchor remodeling is the key target of CTD, independently of PP2A and PP1 activities. Moreover, experiments with human cells further suggested that CTD functions through a conserved mechanism in higher eukaryotes. Altogether, we conclude that CTD induces cytotoxicity by targeting Cdc1 activity in GPI-anchor remodeling in the ER.","affiliation":"From the Laboratory of Chromatin Biology, Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Science Education and Research Bhopal, 462066 Madhya Pradesh, India.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"DST | Science and Engineering Research Board","grantId":"EMR/2015/001797","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Hela Cells"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Endoplasmic Reticulum","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cantharidin","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PD","qualifierName":"pharmacology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Glycosylphosphatidylinositols","meshQualifierLi