asmic reticulum to the cell surface.","authorString":"Muñiz M, Riezman H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Muñiz M","lastName":"Muñiz","firstName":"Manuel","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8011-6991"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Departamento de Biología Celular, Hospital Universitario Virgen del Rocío/CSIC/Universidad de Sevilla, Seville, Spain Universidad de Sevilla and Instituto de Biomedicina de Sevilla (IBiS), Hospital Universitario Virgen del Rocío/CSIC/Universidad de Sevilla, Seville, Spain mmuniz@us.es."}]}},{"fullName":"Riezman H","lastName":"Riezman","firstName":"Howard","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4680-9422"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Centre of Competence in Research (NCCR) Chemical Biology, Department of Biochemistry, University of Geneva, Geneva, Switzerland."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8011-6991"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4680-9422"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"57","journalIssueId":2380389,"dateOfPublication":"2016 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2016,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Lipid Res","title":"Journal of lipid research","essn":"1539-7262","issn":"0022-2275","isoabbreviation":"J Lipid Res","nlmid":"0376606"},"printPublicationDate":"2016-03-01"},"pubYear":"2016","pageInfo":"352-360","abstractText":"In eukaryotes, many cell surface proteins are attached to the plasma membrane via a glycolipid glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor. GPI-anchored proteins (GPI-APs) receive the GPI anchor as a conserved posttranslational modification in the lumen of the endoplasmic reticulum (ER). After anchor attachment, the GPI anchor is structurally remodeled to function as a transport signal that actively triggers the delivery of GPI-APs from the ER to the plasma membrane, via the Golgi apparatus. The structure and composition of the GPI anchor confer a special mode of interaction with membranes of GPI-APs within the lumen of secretory organelles that lead them to be differentially trafficked from other secretory membrane proteins. In this review, we examine the mechanisms by which GPI-APs are selectively transported through the secretory pathway, with special focus on the recent progress made in their actively regulated export from the ER and the trans-Golgi network.","affiliation":"Departamento de Biología Celular, Hospital Universitario Virgen del Rocío/CSIC/Universidad de Sevilla, Seville, Spain Universidad de Sevilla and Instituto de Biomedicina de Sevilla (IBiS), Hospital Universitario Virgen del Rocío/CSIC/Universidad de Sevilla, Seville, Spain mmuniz@us.es.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Endoplasmic Reticulum","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"trans-Golgi Network","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Glycosylphosphatidylinositols","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Membrane Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Protein Transport"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Glycolipid Anchor Remodeling","Lipid-based Sorting","P24 Complex"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Membrane Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Glycosylphosphatidylinositols","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"http://www.jlr.org/content/57/3/352.full.pdf"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1194/jlr.R062760"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC4767001"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC4767001?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1194/jlr.r062760"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":86,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2016-12-13","dateOfCreation":"2015-10-10","dateOfRevision":"2021-02-17","electronicPublicationDate":"2015-10-08","firstPublicationDate":"2015-10-08","firstIndexDate":"2015-10-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2020-07-08","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"21603271","source":"MED","pmid":"21603271","pmcid":"PMC3092604","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC3092604"]},"doi":"10.4061/2011/648159","title":"The Sphingolipid Biosynthetic Pathway Is a Potential Target for Chemotherapy against Chagas Disease.","authorString":"Koeller CM, Heise N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Koeller CM","lastName":"Koeller","firstName":"Carolina Macedo","initials":"CM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1919-7582"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Instituto de Biofísica Carlos Chagas Filho, Universidade Federal do Rio de Janeiro, Centro de Ciências da Saúde, Bloco G-019, Cidade Universitária-Ilha do Fundão, 21941-902 Rio de Janeiro RJ, Brazil."}]}},{"fullName":"Heise N","lastName":"Heise","firstName":"Norton","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0154-7406"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1919-7582"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0154-7406"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"2011","journalIssueId":1807919,"dateOfPublication":"2011 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2011,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Enzyme Res","title":"Enzyme research","essn":"2090-0414","issn":"2090-0406","isoabbreviation":"Enzyme Res","nlmid":"101536666"},"printPublicationDate":"2011-01-01"},"pubYear":"2011","pageInfo":"648159","abstractText":"The protozoan parasite Trypanosoma cruzi is the causative agent of human Chagas disease, for which there currently is no cure. The life cycle of T. cruzi is complex, including an extracellular phase in the triatomine insect vector and an obligatory intracellular stage inside the vertebrate host. These phases depend on a variety of surface glycosylphosphatidylinositol-(GPI-) anchored glycoconjugates that are synthesized by the parasite. Therefore, the surface expression of GPI-anchored components and the biosynthetic pathways of GPI anchors are attractive targets for new therapies for Chagas disease. We identified new drug targets for chemotherapy by taking the available genome sequence information and searching for differences in the sphingolipid biosynthetic pathways (SBPs) of mammals and T. cruzi. In this paper, we discuss the major steps of the SBP in mammals, yeast and T. cruzi, focusing on the IPC synthase and ceramide remodeling of T. cruzi as potential therapeutic targets for Chagas disease.","affiliation":"Instituto de Biofísica Carlos Chagas Filho, Universidade Federal do Rio de Janeiro, Centro de Ciências da Saúde, Bloco G-019, Cidade Universitária-Ilha do Fundão, 21941-902 Rio de Janeiro RJ, Brazil.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","grantId":"477124/2009-7","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado do Rio de Janeiro","grantId":"477124/2009-7","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","grantId":"E-26/110.917/2009","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado do Rio de Janeiro","grantId":"E-26/110.917/2009","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.4061/2011/648159"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC3092604"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC3092604?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":16,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["uniprot"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2011-07-14","dateOfCreation":"2011-05-24","dateOfRevision":"2021-10-20","electronicPublicationDate":"2011-04-19","firstPublicationDate":"2011-04-19","firstIndexDate":"2011-05-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-05","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"26563290","source":"MED","pmid":"26563290","pmcid":"PMC4689344","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC4689344"]},"doi":"10.1194/jlr.r063313","title":"Biosynthesis of GPI-anchored proteins: special emphasis on GPI lipid remodeling.","authorString":"Kinoshita T, Fujita M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kinoshita T","lastName":"Kinoshita","firstName":"Taroh","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"WPI Immunology Frontier Research Center and Research Institute for Microbial Diseases, Osaka University, Suita, Osaka 565-0871, Japan tkinoshi@biken.osaka-u.ac.jp."}]}},{"fullName":"Fujita M","lastName":"Fujita","firstName":"Morihisa","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Key Laboratory of Carbohydrate Chemistry and Biotechnology, Ministry of Education, School of Biotechnology, Jiangnan University, Wuxi, Jiangsu 214122, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data","related_data","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"57","journalIssueId":2358020,"dateOfPublication":"2016 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2016,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Lipid Res","title":"Journal of lipid research","essn":"1539-7262","issn":"0022-2275","isoabbreviation":"J Lipid Res","nlmid":"0376606"},"printPublicationDate":"2016-01-01"},"pubYear":"2016","pageInfo":"6-24","abstractText":"Glycosylphosphatidylinositols (GPIs) act as membrane anchors of many eukaryotic cell surface proteins. GPIs in various organisms have a common backbone consisting of ethanolamine phosphate (EtNP), three mannoses (Mans), one non-N-acetylated glucosamine, and inositol phospholipid, whose structure is EtNP-6Manα-2Manα-6Manα-4GlNα-6myoinositol-P-lipid. The lipid part is either phosphatidylinositol of diacyl or 1-alkyl-2-acyl form, or inositol phosphoceramide. GPIs are attached to proteins via an amide bond between the C-terminal carboxyl group and an amino group of EtNP. Fatty chains of inositol phospholipids are inserted into the outer leaflet of the plasma membrane. More than 150 different human proteins are GPI anchored, whose functions include enzymes, adhesion molecules, receptors, protease inhibitors, transcytotic transporters, and complement regulators. GPI modification imparts proteins with unique characteristics, such as association with membrane microdomains or rafts, transient homodimerization, release from the membrane by cleavage in the GPI moiety, and apical sorting in polarized cells. GPI anchoring is essential for mammalian embryogenesis, development, neurogenesis, fertilization, and immune system. Mutations in genes involved in remodeling of the GPI lipid moiety cause human diseases characterized by neurological abnormalities. Yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae has >60 GPI-anchored proteins (GPI-APs). GPI is essential for growth of yeast. In this review, we discuss biosynthesis of GPI-APs in mammalian cells and yeast with emphasis on the lipid moiety.","affiliation":"WPI Immunology Frontier Research Center and Research Institute for Microbial Diseases, Osaka University, Suita, Osaka 565-0871, Japan tkinoshi@biken.osaka-u.ac.jp.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Membrane","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Membrane Microdomains","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Peroxisomes","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Glycosylphosphati